['Here's how you might implement this in C. This code assumes that the memory locations are valid, which may not be true for your actual scenario, but it demonstrates how you could write a function like this in C. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void copyBytes(char* dest, const char* src, size_t num) {
    if (dest == NULL || src == NULL) {
        printf("Error: Memory location not accessible.\n");
        return;
    }

    for (size_t i = 0; i < num; ++i) {
        dest[i] = src[i];
    }
}
```

In this function, `dest` is a pointer to the destination memory location, `src` is a pointer to the source memory location and `num` is the number of bytes to be copied. The for loop iterates over each byte in the range from 0 to `num-1` (inclusive) and copies it from the source to the destination.

Please note that this code doesn't do any boundary checking, so if you attempt to copy more bytes than are available from your source, or write past the end of your buffer, you will get undefined behavior - in practice, it often means your program may crash or behave unpredictably. 

Also, please be aware that copying memory can cause problems due to data type alignment issues and possible overlapping problems if the memory locations overlap. You should ensure that these potential pitfalls are properly handled by your actual use case. It is also important to ensure that the source buffer has been initialized or else the destination will get garbage values. 

Also note that this code does not do any error checking, so it assumes all pointers provided are valid. For a robust implementation you would want to add checks for these scenarios.
']

['Here's how you can implement this in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void mem_copy(char *destination, char *source, size_t num) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < num; ++i) {
        destination[i] = source[i];
    }
}

int main() {
    char message[] = "Hello World!";
    char buffer[13];  // The size of the buffer should be at least equal to the length of the string plus one for null terminator.

    mem_copy(buffer, message, sizeof(message));

    printf("Message copied: %s\n", buffer);
    return 0;
}
```
In this code snippet, we first define a function `mem_copy` that copies a number of bytes from one memory location to another. Then in the `main` function, we declare and initialize a string (message), and also a buffer where we are going to copy our message. The `sizeof(message)` is used here for simplicity, but you can always provide your own size. After that, we call `mem_copy` with these two strings along with their length as arguments, then print out the copied message.
']

['Here's an example in C language. In this case, we will use `memcpy()` function which is provided by the standard library for copying memory contents from one location to another.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main () {
   const char src[50] = "Hello";
   char dest[50];

   printf("Before memcpy dest = %s\n", dest);
   memcpy(dest, src, strlen(src)+1);
   printf("After memcpy dest = %s\n", dest);

   return(0);
}
```

This program declares a source string `src` and a destination string `dest`. It then uses the function `memcpy()` to copy the contents of `src` into `dest`, which will overwrite any data previously in `dest`. The third argument to `memcpy()` is the number of bytes to be copied, in this case it's the length of `src` plus 1 for the null character ('\0').

After running this program, `printf()` will print "Hello" as both source and destination are identical now.

Remember that C strings are arrays of characters with a terminating '\0'. Because we need to copy the terminating '\0' in addition to the string contents when using `memcpy()` to copy a string, it is necessary to include the length of the string including the null character. The function `strlen(src)` returns the number of characters before the null character in the source string so we add 1 to account for the null character when determining how much memory to allocate.
']

