['You can use rand() function from stdlib.h to generate random numbers and % operator to ensure that the generated number is larger than a given number (e.g., 1000). Here's the C code for generating three random integers, printing them and their sum using int and long long data types:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Initialize the seed for rand() function with current time to get different results each time program is run
    srand(time(0));

    int a = 1000 + (rand() % 9998); // Random number between 1000 and 20000 (excluding the upper limit)
    int b = 1000 + (rand() % 9998); // Random number between 1000 and 20000 (excluding the upper limit)
    int c = 1000 + (rand() % 9998); // Random number between 1000 and 20000 (excluding the upper limit)

    printf("a : %d \n", a);
    printf("b : %d \n", b);
    printf("c : %d \n", c);

    int sum = a + b + c;
    long long sum2 = (long long)a + b + c; // casting one of the operands to long long ensures that result is also in long long format.

    printf("sum : %d \n", sum);
    printf("sum2 : %lld \n", sum2);

    return 0;
}
```

Note: This code uses the current system time as seed for the rand() function, which is not ideal in a real-world scenario. If you want to get consistent results, you should use srand(1) instead. But it's generally discouraged to do this in a real world program.
']

['Here is your C code to generate two random numbers, calculate their difference and print out the result:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.time>`

int main() {
    srand(time(NULL)); // Initialize random seed based on current time for random number generation

    int num1 = rand() % 990 + 10; // Generate a random integer between 10 and 1000
    printf("Number 1: %d\n", num1);

    int num2 = rand() % 990 + 10; // Generate a random integer between 10 and 1000
    printf("Number 2: %d\n", num2);

    int difference = abs(num1 - num2); // Calculate the absolute difference between two numbers.
    printf("Difference: %d\n", difference);

    return 0;
}
```
This code uses C standard library's rand() function to generate random integers, and time() function from <stdlib.time> for seeding the random number generator. The abs() function is used to calculate absolute differences between numbers. Please remember that, to use these functionalities, you need to include the right headers (<stdio.h> <stdlib.h> <time.h>), and link against the math library (-lm). 

Also note that rand() generates a pseudo-random number which may not be as random as you want for security or cryptographic purposes. For such cases, it is better to use a more secure random number generation function like arc4random on macOS/iOS and /dev/urandom in Linux.
']

