    for (int i = 0; i < 12; ++i) {
        sales[i] = getMonthlySales();
        printf("Month %d Sales: %d\n", i + 1, sales[i]);

        // Add sales to corresponding quarter in quarterSales array
        if (i <= 2)
            quarterSales[0] += sales[i];
        else if (i <= 5)
            quarterSales[1] += sales[i];
        else if (i <= 8)
            quarterSales[2] += sales[i];
        else
            quarterSales[3] += sales[i];
    }

    // Print the results for each quarter
    printf("\nQuarterly Sales:\n");
    for(int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
        printf("Q%d: %d\n", i + 1, quarterSales[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Please note that you need to link your program with the math library (usually done by including `-lm` in gcc command line), if not already done automatically.

This code uses the rand() function from the standard C library's stdlib.h, which generates pseudo-random numbers using a starting point known as the seed value. The seed is typically set to 1 on program start and every time you call rand(), it modifies this number in some way (usually adds a constant). This means that calling rand() will always return the same sequence of numbers for any given seed, which makes it unsuitable for generating random numbers suitable for security or cryptography.
