['Here's how you could write this in C. This example uses the open(), write(), and close() system calls from the POSIX library.

```C
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    int fd = open("important_config", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, S_IRUSR|S_IWUSR);
    if (fd == -1) {
        /* handle error */
        return 1;
    }

    const char *content = "important_config";
    ssize_t len = write(fd, content, sizeof("important_config")-1);
    if (len < 0 || (size_t)len != sizeof("important_config")-1) {
        /* handle error */
        return 2;
    }

    int rc = close(fd);
    if (rc == -1) {
        /* handle error */
        return 3;
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This code first opens the file `important_config` with the flags `O_WRONLY | O_CREAT` and permissions `S_IRUSR|S_IWUSR`. If this fails, it returns an error code of 1. Then it writes the string `"important_config"` to the file. If this also fails, it returns a different error code (2). Finally, it closes the file and if that fails, it again returns an error code (3).

Note: You need to include the necessary libraries for open(), write() and close(). Also note that the sizeof operator doesn't count the null terminator of the string. So we subtract 1 from its size before passing it to write(). If this does not match the actual number of bytes written, then an error occurred (3).
']

