```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s string\n", argv[0]);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    char *input = argv[1];
    int len = strlen(input);

    // Remove trailing whitespace characters
    while (isspace((unsigned char) input[--len])) {
        input[len] = 0;
    }

    printf("%s\n", input);
    return 0;
}
```
This program first checks if there is exactly one command line argument. If not, it prints a usage message and exits with an error code. Otherwise, it trims trailing whitespace characters from the input string by over-writing them with `\0` characters. Finally, it prints the modified string to the console.
']

